 "lead": 2,
 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "Strasbourg, 1518."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "Four hundred people danced in the streets, for weeks."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "Their shoes wore through. Their feet bled."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "And they could not stop."
  },
  {
   "beat": "host",
   "text": "This isn't a bedtime story. It's in the history books."
  },
  {
   "beat": "host",
   "text": "They called it the Dancing Plague."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "It started on a normal summer day, in July."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "A woman named Frau Troffea walked out of her house, into the middle of the street, and started to dance."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "No music. No band. No wine. Just dancing."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "The neighbours laughed. Maybe her husband had finally left, and she was celebrating."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "But she didn't dance for five minutes."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "She danced day and night, for almost a week."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Her shoes split. Her feet bled. She cried that she couldn't stop. But her legs kept going."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "And then, it spread."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "Within a week, thirty more people had joined her."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "Within a month, some accounts say four hundred were dancing in the square."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "And nobody was having fun."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "They screamed. They begged for help. But their arms and legs kept moving on their own."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "So what did the doctors and the city council do?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "They said the only cure was more dancing. Let them dance it out."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "So they built a wooden stage, and paid musicians with drums and pipes to play, day and night."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Think about that."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "That's like seeing your barn on fire, and throwing more wood on it, so it burns out faster."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Of course, it went badly."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "In the summer heat, starving and exhausted, people began to collapse."
  },
  {
   "beat": "cure",
   "text": "Chroniclers wrote that some of them died. One even claimed fifteen a day, though historians still argue about it."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "So why did it happen?"
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "One idea: their rye bread was poisoned by a fungus called ergot."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "Ergot contains a chemical related to L S D. It causes spasms and wild hallucinations."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "But most historians favour the second idea."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "The people were starving and terrified, and they believed Saint Vitus could curse you to dance until you dropped."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "Fear spread like a disease. Today we call it mass hysteria."
  },
  {
   "beat": "why",
   "text": "In the end, the dancers were taken to a mountain shrine of Saint Vitus, to pray. And slowly, it stopped."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "So next time your feet hurt after a wedding, remember Strasbourg."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "Some of them danced for two months. That's real."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "Follow for more strange history."
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "July 1518, Strasbourg: Frau Troffea danced in the street for days; about 34 joined within a week; chroniclers claim up to 400 within a month; the council prescribed more dancing (guild halls, a wooden stage, paid musicians); some deaths reported, '15 a day' is one later claim and disputed; dancers taken to a shrine of St Vitus near Saverne; the outbreak faded by September.",
   "working": "July to early September is about two months.",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Dancing plague of 1518; John Waller, A Time to Dance, a Time to Die (2008); Smithsonian; History.com"
  },
  {
   "claim": "Ergot (Claviceps purpurea) on rye produces ergot alkaloids, including lysergic acid, the precursor of LSD; ergotism causes convulsions and hallucinations. Many historians (e.g. Waller) favour mass psychogenic illness driven by famine, disease and belief in St Vitus' curse.",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Waller 2008; Britannica, ergotism"
  }
 ],
